/-!
# RFC 8032 §7.1 — Ed25519 Known-Answer Test vectors

Canonical Lean-side declaration of the Ed25519 KAT vectors. The bench
(`bench_ed25519_sign.c` in catcrypt-bench, `KATS[]`)
must hold the same bytes; the audit script
`scripts/check_kat_consistency.sh` diffs them.

## Why this lives in Lean rather than only the bench

PBT (`PbtAxiomCheck.lean`) catches transcription errors in *axiom shapes*
but cannot exercise the full sign/verify path because Ed25519's field
arithmetic is opaque on the Lean side. The 3 RFC §7.1 vectors below are
the wire-level integration spec — bytes flow in, bytes flow out, and the
mapping is fixed by RFC 8032 §7.1. Owning the bytes here means:

1. Any change to the bench KAT is audited against this file (versioned,
   diffable, signed-off).
2. When AUCurves Phase 1.5 lands (Coq → Rust extraction), the same bytes
   become the conformance gate for the certified path.
3. The bytes themselves are subject to structural sanity checks via
   `decide` (lengths, indices) at elaboration time — typos surface
   without rebuilding the bench.

## Skipped vectors

RFC 8032 §7.1 has five total vectors (test1, test2, test3, test1024,
testabc). The bench currently exercises the three short ones. test1024
(1023-byte msg) and testabc (SHA-512 of "abc") are valuable for SHA-512
input-length coverage; including them here is open work — see "Open
notes" at the bottom.
-/

namespace CatCrypt.Crypto.Jasmin.Ed25519.KAT

/-- One Ed25519 KAT vector: seed, derived public key, message, expected
    signature. Bytes are big-endian as printed in RFC 8032 §7.1. -/
structure KatVec where
  name   : String
  seed   : ByteArray  -- 32 bytes
  pk     : ByteArray  -- 32 bytes
  msg    : ByteArray
  sig    : ByteArray  -- 64 bytes

/-- Helper: build a `ByteArray` from a literal `List UInt8`. -/
def ofBytes (xs : List UInt8) : ByteArray :=
  ⟨xs.toArray⟩

/-- TEST 1 — empty message. RFC 8032 §7.1 first vector. -/
def test1 : KatVec where
  name := "TEST 1 — empty msg"
